// Trunc non-commutation, power-of-ten argument: x = -10^-m. Trunc rounds toward ZERO, and expm1(x) < 0 while exp(x) in (0,1) is positive, so the two forms round the SAME residual in OPPOSITE directions - Trunc(exp(x)) - 1 is the floor, Trunc(expm1(x)) the ceiling, one ULP apart whenever the residual is nonzero (the nearest and directed modes all commute, because expm1 and exp differ by exactly 1, an exact multiple of the ULP; only the sign-dependent modes do not). Here expm1(-10^-m) = -10^-m + (1/2)*10^-2m sits strictly between the grid points -10^-m and 0 at S = m, so the correct Trunc answer is exactly 0 while a composed exp-minus-one returns -10^-m - the starkest form of the split, and the reason expm1 exists as its own function rather than a composition. HalfTowardZero splits the same way at a tie. At the row's OWN scale S = m no depth bound applies - non-exactness is carried by the 9-run immediately below the LSB, not by a deep deciding digit - but that is not the only scale it is graded at. A row is graded at EVERY cell whose envelope holds it, not only the one it was derived for, so the binding constraint is that the first deciding digit BELOW the LSB must fall inside the 1233 stored digits at every such cell - at a far deeper scale that is a much later series term. Inputs failing that anywhere were dropped; what remains is decidable at every cell it reaches. Series arm. Arguments already listed above as tie arguments are not repeated; the split applies to them at S = m just the same
